    {
      "sectionId": "section-disclaimer",
      "heading": "Understanding RPGBOT’s Rating System",
      "sourceAnchor": "disclaimer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "RPGBOT uses a color coding scheme which is simple to understand and easy to read at a glance. This system is designed to help players make informed decisions for their dnd character builds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Red"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Bad, useless options, or options which\n\t\tare extremely situational. Nearly never useful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Orange"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": OK options, or useful options\n\t\tthat only apply in rare circumstances. Useful sometimes."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Green"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Good options. Useful often."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Blue"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Fantastic options, often essential\n\t\tto the function of your character. Useful very frequently."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-why-do-we-care-about-rituals",
      "heading": "Why do we care about rituals?",
      "sourceAnchor": "why-do-we-care-about-rituals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-why-do-we-care-about-rituals-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Ritual spells are cast without expending a spell slot, making them excellent utility options outside of combat. These spells run most of the level range, and include fantastic utility options like Alarm and Leomund’s Tiny Hut. Having these options available to the party can solve a lot of problems with little resource cost, so having ritual casters in the party can solve a lot of problems for little cost."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-who-can-already-cast-rituals",
      "heading": "Who Can Already Cast Rituals?",
      "sourceAnchor": "who-can-already-cast-rituals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-who-can-already-cast-rituals-1",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Artificer: Similar to the wizard, but due to their reduced spellcasting progression they get new ritual spells slower than full casters or characters with the Ritual Caster feat."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Cleric / Druid: With access to their full spell list, any ritual on their spell list is available, "
              },
              {
                "type": "emphasis",
                "text": "provided that you prepared it"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ". If you didn’t bring whatever ritual you need, you’ll need to prepare it the next day."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Warlocks with Book of Ancient Secrets: While Book of Ancient Secrets does cost some build decisions (your pact and an invocation), it’s also the best version of Ritual Casting available. You collect spells like an artificer or wizard, but the spells aren’t restricted by spell list, so the entire world of ritual spells is available to you."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Wizard: The most iconic ritual caster, the Wizard can cast any ritual spell written in their spellbook, making many of the best rituals in the game available to the Wizard at a moment’s notice without the need to prepare those spells."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
