    {
      "sectionId": "section-the-ritual-caster-feat",
      "heading": "The Ritual Caster Feat",
      "sourceAnchor": "the-ritual-caster-feat",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-the-ritual-caster-feat-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The feat itself is actually very simple. The decision point buried in the feat’s benefits is not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-the-ritual-caster-feat-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Ritual Caster gives you a ritual book and the ability to write spells into it. You can use rituals of a level that a full caster of the same level could cast, so don’t get ideas of casting extremely high-level ritual spells to get around level restrictions. The benefit of the feat is to get ritual casting on a character that doesn’t already have it or of a type which you don’t already have."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-the-ritual-caster-feat-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "When you take the feat, you must pick a spellcasting class from which to take your rituals, and you are permanently bound to that decision. As such, you need to pick a class that both matches up with your ability scores (you need 13 in that class’s casting ability) and which has the spells you want."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-the-ritual-caster-feat-4",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Bard"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": The only viable option if\n\t\tCharisma is your only qualifying ability score. A weird mix of druid and\n\t\twizard rituals. Some of them are good, but I don’t think they’re worth a\n\t\tfeat."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Cleric"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Exclusive access to some great\n\t\tdivination rituals like Augury and Divination, as well as cool things like\n\t\tCeremony and Forbiddance. If you have cleric rituals and wizard rituals, you\n\t\thave every ritual worth having."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Druid"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Roughly half of the Druid’s\n\t\trituals overlap with the Cleric, and the remaining rituals are all related\n\t\tto interacting with beasts, which becomes much less important when they\n\t\tmostly fall out of the CR range around level 4 and then vanish entirely by\n\t\tlevel 10."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Sorcerer"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Only 4 rituals, and with the\n\t\texception of Water Walk they’re all available to the Wizard. If you need\n\t\trituals but Charisma is your only stat above 13, go for the Bard."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Warlock"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Only 4 rituals, and they’re all\n\t\tavailable to the Wizard. If you need rituals but Charisma is your only stat\n\t\tabove 13, go for the Bard."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Wizard"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": More rituals than anyone else, and\n\t\talso the best rituals. With the exception of a handful of cleric-only\n\t\trituals, you could take Wizard and never worry about ritual casting\n\t\tagain."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-good-candidates-for-ritual-caster",
      "heading": "Good Candidates for Ritual Caster",
      "sourceAnchor": "good-candidates-for-ritual-caster",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-good-candidates-for-ritual-caster-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "While Ritual Caster doesn’t feature prominently in our example builds, it’s a great addition to many classes and subclasses which are already set up to qualify for the feat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-good-candidates-for-ritual-caster-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Arcane Trickster Rogue: You already need a bit of Intelligence for your spells, and adding wizard rituals can expand your already massive toolbox."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "link",
                "text": "Eldritch Knight Fighter",
                "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/fighter/subclasses/eldritch-knight/"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": You already need a bit of Intelligence for your spells, and Ritual Caster adds some much-needed utility to a class that’s basically all combat."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Monk: Cleric rituals will add some utility to the Monk, but since it doesn’t address any of the Monk’s fundamental challenges it’s hard to fit into a build."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Paladin: Bard rituals are decent, but may not be good enough to justify on a class that’s MAD and already struggles to fit feats into a build."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Ranger: Cleric rituals (not druid rituals) add a lot to the Ranger. Druid makes more sense for flavor, but cleric rituals are generally more impactful."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-conclusion",
      "heading": "Conclusion",
      "sourceAnchor": "conclusion",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-conclusion-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "I won’t say that Wizards are the best (I mean, I will, but it won’t be right here), but in terms of Ritual Caster there’s a pretty clear winner here, with the Cleric coming in a close second, and every other option lagging pretty far behind. If your party lacks ritual spellcasting, Ritual Caster is a fantastic way to get it, and knowing what’s already available within your party compared to what’s available makes it much easier to benefit from the feat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
